WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:07.330
Hey Wisecrack, Jared here.

00:00:07.330 --> 00:00:09.160
Today we’re talking about everyone’s favorite
psychopath.

00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:12.430
A man who is one twirly moustache away from being

00:00:12.430 --> 00:00:13.430
a hipster.

00:00:13.430 --> 00:00:15.380
The mayor of pee-pee pants city - Negan.

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:21.300
And yes, there are spoilers ahead.

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:25.580
Despite being the latest antagonist of the
show, Negan gets an inordinate amount of praise

00:00:25.580 --> 00:00:26.599
from fans.

00:00:26.599 --> 00:00:30.319
Negan is hard not to love, in part because
he’s just so damn charming.

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:39.760
It might not be just his undeniable charisma that
makes us gravitate towards him.

00:00:39.770 --> 00:00:43.190
He is one of the most effective leaders of the
post-apocalypse.

00:00:43.190 --> 00:00:45.650
But the real question is: how effective, exactly??

00:00:45.650 --> 00:00:49.870
Sure, we could leave it as “not very,”
because of course someone is going to murder

00:00:49.870 --> 00:00:54.250
or overthrow him, ((why?)) cause, well, this has
all happened before.

00:00:54.250 --> 00:00:55.250
But I digress.

00:00:55.250 --> 00:00:57.810
Negan’s cruelty is neither novel, nor irrational.

00:00:57.810 --> 00:01:02.440
With a little help from philosophy and psychology,
we can better understand how Negan is the

00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:03.320
perfect dictator.

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:12.300
A leader, especially a post-apocalyptic one,
can spend all day putting people in their

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:17.020
place, rooting out insurrection as it appears,
and awing people with the occasional spectacle

00:01:17.030 --> 00:01:18.050
of violence.

00:01:18.050 --> 00:01:22.600
But a much more effective dictator can get
people to police themselves, and Negan is

00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:24.320
a master of this craft.

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:29.600
Negan is terrifying, as many dictators are,
but what defines his terror is his seeming

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:30.600
omnipresence.

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:33.860
He’s everywhere, and always watching.

00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:41.250
In fact, the finale of season 6 is like one
giant proof of concept for this: Negan’s

00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:45.060
Saviors let Rick’s gang believe they’ve
somehow evaded them, with subtle reminders

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:47.270
that they're always one step ahead of them.

00:01:47.270 --> 00:01:54.880
Negan even admits that the whole thing was orchestrated to strike fear into them.

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:02.600
Negan’s method isn’t so different from
a system devised by English philosopher Jeremy

00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:06.580
Bentham for prisons: a design that would make
it impossible for a prisoner to know whether

00:02:06.580 --> 00:02:10.670
or not the guards were watching him: the pre-electronic
equivalent of CCTV.

00:02:10.670 --> 00:02:15.230
It was called the panopticon, from the Greek
term for “all-seeing.”

00:02:15.230 --> 00:02:19.450
French philosopher Michel Foucault said that
Bentham’s panopticon was “power reduced

00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:22.750
to its ideal form,” a kind of “political
technology.”

00:02:22.750 --> 00:02:27.640
When people are always, but maybe not, under
surveillance, they internalize disciplinary

00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:30.349
power, they become their own police.

00:02:30.349 --> 00:02:39.450
Negan takes panoptic power and infuses it
with his own brand of Lucille-infused terror.

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:44.080
Daryl tries to escape, but soon realizes it
was all a ploy - the door was left unlocked

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:48.130
to encourage Daryl’s escape, only to be
surrounded by Negan when he’s on the cusp

00:02:48.130 --> 00:02:48.890
of freedom.

00:02:51.569 --> 00:02:56.890
On one level, this is pure psychological manipulation:
causing Daryl, and the viewer, to question

00:02:56.890 --> 00:03:00.110
reality: is this a chance to escape, or an
elaborate trap?

00:03:00.110 --> 00:03:03.879
It also inculcates in Daryl this kind of panoptic
terror.

00:03:03.879 --> 00:03:10.140
Negan is always watching, whether you realize
it or not- so you best stay in line.

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:14.980
It’s for this reason that Negan is so obsessed
with finding the missing guns from Alexandria.

00:03:16.540 --> 00:03:20.140
Sure, it’s part “I don’t want Team Rick
to use them to shoot me,” but it’s also

00:03:20.140 --> 00:03:23.840
to further ingrain the point: you can’t
hide anything from Negan.

00:03:25.530 --> 00:03:28.710
Even saviors are constantly fearful for what
Negan will find out.

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:35.200
Negan’s followers saying “I am Negan”
reinforces this constant surveillance.

00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:41.720
There’s no one to confide in, no one to
trust, because they all have become little

00:03:41.730 --> 00:03:43.130
satellites of surveillance.

00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:48.800
Even we, the viewer, have managed to internalize
Negan’s terror.

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:51.940
Reeling from the loss of the one of the few
characters we still gave a shit about, we

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:55.580
we all collectively clench our buttholes when people
break the rules.

00:03:55.590 --> 00:03:59.510
Negan has achieved the goals Bentham’s panopticon
without the actual technology.

00:03:59.510 --> 00:04:02.510
He “breaks” people with his campaign of
terror.

00:04:04.950 --> 00:04:08.520
To “break” someone is to internalize Negan’s
set of rules.

00:04:08.520 --> 00:04:12.300
Bentham’s panopticon doesn’t “break”
prisoners in the same terrifying way, but

00:04:12.300 --> 00:04:17.100
the goal is the same: to make people police
themselves, to behave as if someone else is

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:19.660
watching, whether or not they actually are.

00:04:23.320 --> 00:04:27.400
The perfect example of this is Dwight, who
admits to Daryl that he, too, thought he would

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:28.830
never kneel to Negan.

00:04:28.830 --> 00:04:34.750
Now, he fully embodies Negan’s rules, despite
the fact he shows clear signs of hating him.

00:04:34.750 --> 00:04:39.440
But violence and surveillance alone don’t
provide the best means for controlling people.

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:43.460
People find ways to be unseen, and even when
they’re being watched, don’t go on performing

00:04:43.460 --> 00:04:45.460
their jobs to the best of their ability.

00:04:45.460 --> 00:04:47.250
But Negan is smarter than that.

00:04:47.250 --> 00:04:52.810
Negan is art 20th Century totalitarian, and
also part 21st Century corporate manager.

00:04:52.810 --> 00:04:54.870
He understands talent development.

00:05:01.720 --> 00:05:06.919
Those brazen enough to defy him are often
spared, while their friends are killed instead;

00:05:06.919 --> 00:05:10.719
their rebellious tendencies are raw talent
for Savior Inc. to develop.

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:17.919
He’s also keenly aware of the dangers of
talentless sycophants, cutting out yes-men

00:05:17.919 --> 00:05:20.030
before they ruin the corporate culture.

00:05:20.030 --> 00:05:25.130
Negan knows the secret sauce of motivation,
and as a result, he does more than just punish

00:05:25.130 --> 00:05:26.580
people, he rewards them.

00:05:26.580 --> 00:05:31.050
Negan’s response to people may seem erratic-
as if his bouts of violence are driven by

00:05:31.050 --> 00:05:34.949
his temper, but I’d like to argue that it’s
all very deliberate.

00:05:34.949 --> 00:05:39.639
He’s developed a whole points system to
incentivize work, and while consistent rewards

00:05:39.639 --> 00:05:46.830
are a great way to motivate people, psychologists
argue there’s an even better way to get

00:05:46.830 --> 00:05:49.410
people to do shit: reward them randomly.

00:05:49.410 --> 00:05:53.740
Behavioral psychologists have found that when
rewards are unexpected, and unscheduled, they

00:05:53.741 --> 00:05:56.669
are far more effective in promoting certain
behaviors.

00:05:56.669 --> 00:06:01.110
Don’t just buy a worker a cup of coffee
every time he hits a deadline, or every 5th

00:06:01.120 --> 00:06:02.960
time, make it a surprise.

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:06.000
If you don’t believe me: go play a slot
machine,.

00:06:06.009 --> 00:06:09.169
Random bonuses and treasures will keep you
playing for hours.

00:06:09.169 --> 00:06:11.409
And this is what Negan does all the time.

00:06:17.740 --> 00:06:22.480
There’s also Carl’s stay with Negan, where
it’s not only physical rewards, but emotional

00:06:22.490 --> 00:06:24.650
niceties that are randomly doled out.

00:06:24.650 --> 00:06:29.770
Carl kills Negan’s people, and expects to die,
or be mutilated, but instead Negan takes him

00:06:29.770 --> 00:06:32.610
in and commends his bad-assery.

00:06:32.610 --> 00:06:40.340
But not to overdo the kindness, he makes Carl
take off his eye-covering, makes fun of him,

00:06:40.340 --> 00:06:42.480
but then apologizes when it goes too far.

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:51.800
Negan then asks Carl to sing a song at the
threat of violence, and intersperses violent

00:06:51.810 --> 00:06:55.360
bat swings with compliments.

00:06:55.360 --> 00:07:00.350
This strategy, generally and unfortunately,
works, and if Negan seems like an abusive

00:07:00.350 --> 00:07:05.199
boyfriend, he should: this random reinforcement
is also used by abusive partners to keep their

00:07:05.199 --> 00:07:06.579
victims in relationships.

00:07:12.800 --> 00:07:17.740
Another way to understand Negan is through
the lense of sweat-stained t-shirt aficionado Slavoj

00:07:17.750 --> 00:07:20.000
Zizek and his works on totalitarianism.

00:07:30.580 --> 00:07:32.400
This is Negan to a tee.

00:07:32.410 --> 00:07:35.039
The saviors are like a well-ordered anarchy.

00:07:35.039 --> 00:07:39.160
Aside from “the rules” to which Negan
constantly refers, his followers are free

00:07:39.160 --> 00:07:41.180
to murder and pillage.

00:07:41.180 --> 00:07:45.580
According to the Ziz, the totalitarian master,
“tells us that the prohibitions which regulate

00:07:45.580 --> 00:07:51.200
social life and guarantee a minimum of decency
are worthless, just a device to keep the common

00:07:51.200 --> 00:07:56.910
people at bay – we, on the other hand, are
free to let ourselves go, to kill, rape, plunder,

00:07:56.910 --> 00:07:59.560
but only insofar as we follow the master.”

00:07:59.560 --> 00:08:04.040
Through this lens, Negan’s dictatorship
makes perfect sense in context of the real-world

00:08:04.040 --> 00:08:08.880
totalitarian governments: people’s deepest
desires are pandered to, so long as they don’t

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:12.300
conflict with the ruling establishment- Wanna
steal and murder?

00:08:12.300 --> 00:08:12.880
Go to town!

00:08:12.880 --> 00:08:13.820
Fuck the rules.

00:08:13.820 --> 00:08:16.000
Just don’t fuck with MY rules.

00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:20.180
So Negan is a pretty effective dictator, but
is he an effective ruler?

00:08:20.180 --> 00:08:24.870
The problem with heavy-handed dictatorship
is that it breeds problems, the kind of problems

00:08:24.870 --> 00:08:29.280
we’re sure to see as Rick, Ezekial, and
maybe even Dwight, rebel against Negan.

00:08:29.280 --> 00:08:33.769
It’s the same problem of real-life dictatorships
that are constantly threatened by assassination

00:08:33.769 --> 00:08:36.209
plots, insurrections, and so on.

00:08:36.209 --> 00:08:41.000
Maybe Negan should have brushed up on his
Machiavelli who famously said “he who becomes master

00:08:41.009 --> 00:08:45.730
of a city accustomed to freedom and does not
destroy it, may expect to be destroyed by

00:08:45.730 --> 00:08:46.730
it."

00:08:46.730 --> 00:08:47.730
What do you guys think?

00:08:47.730 --> 00:08:52.270
Has Negan perfected the science of control,
or is he sowing the seeds of his own demise?

